Description: zebra: accept a lower EVPN MAC/neigh sequence number from BGP
 zebra keeps a remote MAC or neighbor whose MAC-mobility sequence number is
 higher than the one in an update from bgpd, and ignores the update. After a
 VM moves, bgpd can correctly select a route with a lower sequence number
 than one zebra installed earlier, and zebra then keeps forwarding to the old
 VTEP indefinitely, while BGP shows the right next hop.
 .
 These three upstream commits make zebra accept bgpd's sequence number for a
 remote entry (a local entry that is ready for BGP still keeps the higher
 one). The hidden "no evpn accept-bgp-seq" config command restores the old
 behaviour at runtime.
 .
 Upstream shipped these with a bgpd rework of the EVPN VNI tables in the same
 pull request; only the zebra commits are backported. That rework matters
 when a moved MAC also has MAC+IP routes; here every moving MAC is MAC-only.
